---
/**
 * CA Foundation exam pattern — every figure on this page is rendered from
 * src/data/foundation.js (paperStructure, foundationScoring, passRule,
 * foundationAttempts). Nothing is typed into the body by hand.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import Badge from '../../components/Badge.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import PassRule from '../../components/PassRule.astro';
import TrustRow from '../../components/TrustRow.astro';
import {
  foundationAttempts,
  foundationPapers,
  foundationScoring,
  getPaperStructure,
  passRule,
} from '../../data/foundation.js';

const rows = foundationPapers.map((p) => ({ paper: p, structure: getPaperStructure(p.slug) }));
const objective = rows.filter((r) => r.structure.mode === 'objective');
const subjective = rows.filter((r) => r.structure.mode === 'subjective');
const mcqStructure = objective[0].structure;
const descriptiveStructure = subjective[0].structure;
---

<Base
  title="CA Foundation exam pattern"
  description="The CA Foundation paper pattern under the New Scheme: durations, question structure, OMR and negative marking, the 40/50 passing rule and the attempt calendar with registration cut-offs."
>
  <main class="page">
    <div style="max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
      <Breadcrumb crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Guides', href: '/start-here/' }, { label: 'Foundation exam pattern' }]} />

      <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 30px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em; line-height: 1.25;">
        CA Foundation exam pattern
      </h1>
      <div style="margin: 12px 0 24px;">
        <TrustRow attempt={foundationAttempts[0].name} updated="14 Aug 2026" source="ICAI Foundation syllabus & scheme of examination" />
      </div>

      <div class="article">
        <p>
          Four papers, {passRule.totalMarks} marks in total. Two are written by hand and two are answered
          on an OMR sheet, and the two halves reward completely different exam-day habits. Here is the
          structure as ICAI publishes it.
        </p>

        <h2>The four papers at a glance</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="list-card">
        {rows.map(({ paper, structure }) => (
          <a class="list-row" href={`/foundation/${paper.slug}/`}>
            <span class="row-num tnum">{String(paper.number).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <span style="flex: 1;">
              <span style="display: block; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600;">{paper.name}</span>
              <span style="font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-3);">
                {structure.modeLabel} · {structure.durationLabel} · {structure.marks} marks
              </span>
            </span>
            {structure.negativeMarking
              ? <Badge kind="amended">Negative marking</Badge>
              : <Badge kind="neutral">No negative marking</Badge>}
            <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="article" style="margin-top: 28px;">
        <h2>Papers {subjective.map((r) => r.paper.number).join(' and ')} — the written papers</h2>
        <p>
          {descriptiveStructure.durationLabel}, {descriptiveStructure.marks} marks, answered in a booklet.
          {' '}{descriptiveStructure.questionPattern.summary} That choice matters more than students expect:
          you are marked on {descriptiveStructure.questionPattern.compulsory + descriptiveStructure.questionPattern.chooseFromRemaining.attempt}
          {' '}questions of {descriptiveStructure.questionPattern.marksPerQuestion} marks each, so a single
          badly-chosen question costs a full {descriptiveStructure.questionPattern.marksPerQuestion} marks.
        </p>
        <p>
          Presentation earns marks here — stated provisions, labelled working notes, a conclusion that
          answers the question asked. There is no negative marking on these papers, so an incomplete
          attempt always beats a blank.
        </p>

        <h2>Papers {objective.map((r) => r.paper.number).join(' and ')} — the OMR papers</h2>
        <p>
          {mcqStructure.durationLabel} for {mcqStructure.questionPattern.totalQuestions} questions, which is
          a little over a minute per question. {mcqStructure.questionPattern.summary} You also get
          {' '}{mcqStructure.readingTimeMinutes} minutes of reading time before the clock starts — use it to
          find the questions you can finish quickly, not to start solving.
        </p>

        <Callout kind="pointer" label="Negative marking, priced out">
          <p>
            A correct answer is worth {foundationScoring.correct}. A wrong answer costs
            {' '}{Math.abs(foundationScoring.wrong)}. A blank is {foundationScoring.skipped}. Guessing at
            random across four options therefore breaks about even — it only turns profitable once you can
            rule out two options. This applies to
            {' '}{objective.map((r) => `Paper ${r.paper.number}`).join(' and ')} only; it is the only
            negative marking anywhere in the CA course.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <h2>How Foundation is passed</h2>
      </div>

      <PassRule />

      <div class="article" style="margin-top: 24px;">
        <p>
          Because there is no set-off, the arithmetic that matters is not "how do I top this paper" but
          "is every paper safely clear of {passRule.perPaperPct}, and does the total clear
          {' '}{passRule.aggregateMarks}". A paper you enjoy cannot rescue one you have avoided.
        </p>

        <h2>Attempt calendar and registration cut-offs</h2>
        <p>
          Foundation runs three times a year. Registration closes months before the exam — missing the
          cut-off moves you to the next attempt regardless of how ready you are.
        </p>
      </div>

      {foundationAttempts.map((att) => (
        <div class="card card-pad" style="margin-bottom: 16px;">
          <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline;">
            <strong style="font-size: 17px;">{att.name}</strong>
            <span class="tnum" style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--text-2);">{att.examDates.label}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="list-card" style="margin-top: 14px;">
            {att.papers.map((slot) => {
              const p = foundationPapers.find((fp) => fp.slug === slot.paperSlug);
              return (
                <div class="list-row">
                  <span class="row-num tnum">{String(p.number).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                  <span class="row-title" style="font-weight: 600;">{p.name}</span>
                  <span class="row-meta tnum">{slot.label}</span>
                </div>
              );
            })}
          </div>
          <div style="margin-top: 14px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--text-2); line-height: 1.7;">
            <div><strong style="color: var(--text);">Register by {att.registration.label}</strong> — {att.registration.rule}</div>
            <div>{att.session.label}. {att.dayPattern}</div>
          </div>
          {!att.verified && (
            <div class="trust-row" style="margin-top: 14px;">
              <Badge kind="amended">Confirm before you rely on this</Badge>
              <span>{att.verify[0]}</span>
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}

      <div class="article" style="margin-top: 28px;">
        <h2>Where to go next</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/guides/ca-foundation-weightage/">The official section-wise weightage for all four papers</a> —
          what ICAI has said about how the marks are distributed.<br />
          <a href="/foundation/quantitative-aptitude/">Paper 3, Quantitative Aptitude</a> — the OMR paper most
          students find hardest, with a chapter-by-chapter safe route to {passRule.perPaperPct}.<br />
          <a href="/practice/">Practice with the Foundation scoring rules</a> — the same
          {' '}+{foundationScoring.correct} / {foundationScoring.wrong} arithmetic as the real OMR papers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
